'''''Sedum sexangulare''''', also known as '''tasteless stonecrop''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=SESE6|taxon=Sedum sexangulare|accessdate=9 November 2015}}</ref><ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> is a [[species]] of [[succulent]] [[perennial]] and [[evergreen]] plant of the genus ''[[Sedum]]''. It is similar to ''[[Sedum acre]]'', but has shorter and denser leaves. It gained the binomial name ("six-angled") for its characteristic six spirals of leaves. ''S. sexangulare'' is about 15&nbsp;cm (5&nbsp;in) tall and is native to [[Europe]] and [[Asia]], where it grows in the wild and is often cultivated as an ornamental plant. The plant needs wet or dry sandy soil and a sunny spot. It blooms in June and July with yellow, star-shaped flowers, which attract [[bees]] and other insects.

